<Layout
	title="Texas R-454B Refrigerant Transition Explained | String Prairie"
	description="Confused about the R-410A phaseout and new R-454B / A2L AC units in Texas? Get straight answers on repairs, refrigerant costs, and system replacements in Bastrop."
	breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
	<script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

	<section class="page-header">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/blog">Blog</a> / Industry Updates</div>
			<h1>The Texas R-454B Refrigerant Transition: Straight Answers for Homeowners</h1>
			<p class="post-meta">Updated November 2026 • 5 min read</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<article class="blog-post">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="post-content">
				<p class="lead">
					If you've shopped for a new air conditioner recently or had a technician inspect an older unit in Bastrop County, you've probably heard terms like <strong>R-410A phaseout</strong>, <strong>R-454B</strong>, or <strong>A2L refrigerants</strong>. Here is a plain-English explanation of what changed, what it costs, and whether you actually need to replace your current system.
				</p>

				<h2>Do I Have to Replace My Working R-410A Air Conditioner?</h2>
				<p>
					<strong>No!</strong> You are not legally required to replace a working R-410A system. Under EPA rules, manufacturers transitioned new residential AC and heat pump production to lower-GWP A2L refrigerants (primarily R-454B and R-32), but <strong>R-410A refrigerant and replacement parts remain available</strong> to service existing systems for years to come.
				</p>

				<h2>Can You Mix R-454B and R-410A Parts?</h2>
				<p>
					No. New A2L systems operate at different pressures and include built-in refrigerant leak detection sensors inside the indoor unit. Because of this, you generally cannot pair a new R-454B outdoor condenser with an older R-410A indoor evaporator coil—when replacing major components on an aging system, a full matched replacement is usually the safer, warranty-compliant choice.
				</p>

				<h2>How Does This Affect Repair vs. Replace Decisions?</h2>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Minor repairs (capacitor, contactor, blower motor, drain clog):</strong> Almost always worth repairing on a solid R-410A system. See our <a href="/blog/hvac-repair-costs-bastrop-2026">Bastrop HVAC Repair Cost Guide</a>.</li>
					<li><strong>Major refrigerant leak or failed compressor on a 12+ year old unit:</strong> Because R-410A refrigerant costs will gradually trend upward over time, putting $2,000+ into an old leaking system rarely makes financial sense compared to a new warrantied system.</li>
					<li><strong>Still running an R-22 ("Freon") unit?</strong> If your AC is 15–20+ years old and uses R-22, upgrading to a modern system will slash your summer electric bills and eliminate expensive refrigerant top-offs.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Why New A2L Systems Cost Slightly More</h2>
				<p>
					New R-454B / R-32 equipment includes updated safety sensors, control boards, and higher efficiency SEER2 coils. While upfront prices rose slightly across the industry, newer systems are built for stronger cooling performance and better humidity removal during 100°F+ Bastrop County summers.
				</p>

				<div class="cta-box">
					<h3>Get an Honest Repair-vs-Replace Assessment</h3>
					<p>
						We never use refrigerant rules as a scare tactic to sell you a new unit. Whether you need straightforward <a href="/services/ac-repair">AC repair</a> or a free quote on a new <a href="/services/ac-installation">AC installation</a>, String Prairie gives you the honest facts.
					</p>
					<div class="cta-buttons">
						<a
							href="https://book.housecallpro.com/book/String-Prairie-Heating--Air/df82ded280ce43f4a9ee6ee6d36aef76?v2=true"
							target="_blank"
